Commit 69be843f fixed a JIT-only leak: the `optin`/`optout`-optional codegen branch suppressed the issue for an absent key, but still ran the assignment afterward. If a `superRefine` on that optional key added an issue but also set `ctx.value`, the leaked value landed in a "successful" result, even though the key was absent and validation failed:

```ts
const schema = z.object({
  a: z.string().optional().superRefine((_v, ctx) => {
    ctx.addIssue({ code: "custom", message: "bad" });
    ctx.value = "leaked";
  }),
});
schema.safeParse({}); // JIT: { success: true, data: { a: "leaked" } }
```

Interpreted path (and async) returns early in this case, so no leak — divergence was JIT-only. Fix: make the fastpass branch return early too, matching interpreted-path semantics, instead of falling through to the assignment.
